  "worked_solution": "Since $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, we find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b],$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-5){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & 12\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Hence ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Now, from the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n3{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=-9\\,\u27f9\\,{x}_{2}=-3-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=-1\\,\u27f9\\,{x}_{1}=-1-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the general solution is given by \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1-{x}_{3} \\\\ -3-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=2$, we have\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $T(x)=b.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=-3-(-5)=2.$",
